1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:03,561
In 1965, a young computer scientist wrote

2
00:00:03,561 --> 00:00:06,312
an essay describing the ultimate

3
00:00:06,312 --> 00:00:07,228
display.

4
00:00:07,878 --> 00:00:10,185
"A chair displayed in such a room would

5
00:00:10,185 --> 00:00:12,330
be good enough to sit in," he wrote.

6
00:00:12,980 --> 00:00:15,547
"Handcuffs displayed in such a room would

7
00:00:15,547 --> 00:00:17,942
be confining, and a bullet displayed in

8
00:00:17,942 --> 00:00:19,768
such a room would be fatal."

9
00:00:20,418 --> 00:00:22,346
His name was Ivan Sutherland.

10
00:00:22,346 --> 00:00:24,670
Three years later, he built one of the

11
00:00:24,670 --> 00:00:26,485
first head-mounted displays —

12
00:00:27,135 --> 00:00:29,224
a machine so heavy it had to hang from the

13
00:00:29,224 --> 00:00:29,735
ceiling.

14
00:00:30,385 --> 00:00:32,449
Today, millions of people wear its

15
00:00:32,449 --> 00:00:34,123
descendants on their faces.

16
00:00:34,773 --> 00:00:37,337
And they raise a question philosophers

17
00:00:37,337 --> 00:00:39,900
have asked for two and a half thousand

18
00:00:39,900 --> 00:00:42,276
years: if a world feels completely

19
00:00:42,276 --> 00:00:43,401
real... is it?

20
00:00:44,051 --> 00:00:46,206
In this video, you'll see why people

21
00:00:46,206 --> 00:00:48,526
refuse to step off a virtual ledge they

22
00:00:48,526 --> 00:00:49,741
know isn't there,

23
00:00:50,391 --> 00:00:52,592
how your brain can adopt a fake hand as

24
00:00:52,592 --> 00:00:53,168
its own,

25
00:00:53,818 --> 00:00:56,091
how an avatar can quietly change your

26
00:00:56,091 --> 00:00:57,000
personality —

27
00:00:57,650 --> 00:00:59,825
and why one philosopher's thought

28
00:00:59,825 --> 00:01:02,483
experiment suggests that most of us would

29
00:01:02,483 --> 00:01:05,140
refuse paradise, if it were made of code.

30
00:01:05,790 --> 00:01:07,612
Quick favor before we start:

31
00:01:07,612 --> 00:01:10,139
if you like questions about what's real,

32
00:01:10,139 --> 00:01:12,372
subscribe. It genuinely helps the

33
00:01:12,372 --> 00:01:13,018
channel.

34
00:01:13,668 --> 00:01:16,328
The idea of a convincing fake world is

35
00:01:16,328 --> 00:01:18,210
much older than computers.

36
00:01:18,860 --> 00:01:22,247
Around 375 BC, in The Republic, Plato

37
00:01:22,247 --> 00:01:25,715
imagined prisoners chained inside a cave

38
00:01:25,715 --> 00:01:26,925
since birth.

39
00:01:27,575 --> 00:01:30,152
All they can see are shadows on a wall,

40
00:01:30,152 --> 00:01:32,546
cast by objects carried behind them.

41
00:01:33,196 --> 00:01:34,953
To them, the shadows are reality.

42
00:01:35,603 --> 00:01:38,199
When one prisoner is freed and sees the

43
00:01:38,199 --> 00:01:40,671
real world, the light hurts his eyes.

44
00:01:41,321 --> 00:01:43,315
And when he returns to tell the others,

45
00:01:43,315 --> 00:01:44,549
they don't believe him.

46
00:01:45,199 --> 00:01:47,798
The phrase "virtual reality" itself came

47
00:01:47,798 --> 00:01:49,611
long before the technology.

48
00:01:50,261 --> 00:01:53,029
In 1938, the French playwright Antonin

49
00:01:53,029 --> 00:01:55,929
Artaud described theater as "la réalité

50
00:01:55,929 --> 00:01:58,697
virtuelle" — a world that becomes real

51
00:01:58,697 --> 00:02:01,004
for the audience while it lasts.

52
00:02:01,654 --> 00:02:03,040
The machines came later.

53
00:02:03,690 --> 00:02:06,685
In 1962, cinematographer Morton Heilig

54
00:02:06,685 --> 00:02:09,679
patented the Sensorama — a booth where

55
00:02:09,679 --> 00:02:12,325
you sat, looked into a stereoscopic

56
00:02:12,325 --> 00:02:13,021
screen,

57
00:02:13,671 --> 00:02:16,099
and rode a motorcycle through Brooklyn,

58
00:02:16,099 --> 00:02:18,642
with wind on your face, a vibrating seat,

59
00:02:18,642 --> 00:02:19,740
and even smells.

60
00:02:20,390 --> 00:02:23,378
Then came Sutherland's ceiling-mounted

61
00:02:23,378 --> 00:02:26,585
headset in 1968, nicknamed "The Sword of

62
00:02:26,585 --> 00:02:27,606
Damocles."

63
00:02:28,256 --> 00:02:30,999
In the 1980s, Jaron Lanier and his

64
00:02:30,999 --> 00:02:34,094
company VPL popularized the term "virtual

65
00:02:34,094 --> 00:02:36,908
reality" and sold gloves that let you

66
00:02:36,908 --> 00:02:38,948
reach into digital worlds.

67
00:02:39,598 --> 00:02:42,465
In 1995, Nintendo launched the Virtual

68
00:02:42,465 --> 00:02:45,537
Boy. It gave many players headaches, sold

69
00:02:45,537 --> 00:02:48,541
poorly, and was discontinued in less than

70
00:02:48,541 --> 00:02:49,223
a year.

71
00:02:49,873 --> 00:02:52,208
For a while, VR seemed like a dream that

72
00:02:52,208 --> 00:02:53,294
would never work.

73
00:02:53,944 --> 00:02:56,576
Then, in 2012, a young engineer named

74
00:02:56,576 --> 00:02:59,273
Palmer Luckey launched a Kickstarter

75
00:02:59,273 --> 00:03:01,905
campaign for a headset called Oculus

76
00:03:01,905 --> 00:03:02,497
Rift.

77
00:03:03,147 --> 00:03:05,322
Two years later, Facebook bought the

78
00:03:05,322 --> 00:03:07,609
company for about two billion dollars.

79
00:03:08,259 --> 00:03:10,704
The shadows on the cave wall had become

80
00:03:10,704 --> 00:03:12,160
very, very convincing.

81
00:03:12,810 --> 00:03:14,434
Here's the strange part:

82
00:03:14,434 --> 00:03:16,901
your brain is remarkably easy to fool.

83
00:03:17,551 --> 00:03:19,416
Researchers call it presence — the

84
00:03:19,416 --> 00:03:21,185
feeling of actually being there.

85
00:03:21,835 --> 00:03:24,509
In one well-known experiment at the

86
00:03:24,509 --> 00:03:27,323
University of North Carolina in 2002,

87
00:03:27,323 --> 00:03:30,489
participants walked through a virtual room

88
00:03:30,489 --> 00:03:32,459
that ended in a deep pit.

89
00:03:33,109 --> 00:03:35,337
A real wooden ledge on the floor matched

90
00:03:35,337 --> 00:03:36,373
the virtual edge.

91
00:03:37,023 --> 00:03:38,892
Everyone knew the pit wasn't real.

92
00:03:38,892 --> 00:03:40,458
They were standing in a lab.

93
00:03:41,108 --> 00:03:43,383
And yet their heart rates went up as they

94
00:03:43,383 --> 00:03:45,657
approached the edge — and many refused to

95
00:03:45,657 --> 00:03:46,484
step forward.

96
00:03:47,134 --> 00:03:49,864
In VR labs around the world, a simple

97
00:03:49,864 --> 00:03:52,458
version of this demo is still used:

98
00:03:52,458 --> 00:03:55,051
a narrow plank over a virtual drop.

99
00:03:55,701 --> 00:03:58,268
People who are perfectly calm in a normal

100
00:03:58,268 --> 00:04:00,893
room suddenly crouch, hold their arms out,

101
00:04:00,893 --> 00:04:03,284
and shuffle like they're on a rooftop.

102
00:04:03,934 --> 00:04:05,861
The rational part of you says:

103
00:04:05,861 --> 00:04:08,198
it's not real. An older part of you

104
00:04:08,198 --> 00:04:10,592
says: don't fall. And the older part

105
00:04:10,592 --> 00:04:11,527
usually wins.

106
00:04:12,177 --> 00:04:14,584
And your brain isn't only fooled about the

107
00:04:14,584 --> 00:04:16,991
world. It can be fooled about your body.

108
00:04:17,641 --> 00:04:20,609
In 1998, psychologists Matthew Botvinick

109
00:04:20,609 --> 00:04:23,379
and Jonathan Cohen published a simple

110
00:04:23,379 --> 00:04:25,819
experiment called the rubber hand

111
00:04:25,819 --> 00:04:26,677
illusion.

112
00:04:27,327 --> 00:04:29,880
A participant's real hand is hidden behind

113
00:04:29,880 --> 00:04:32,434
a screen. A rubber hand is placed in front

114
00:04:32,434 --> 00:04:33,058
of them.

115
00:04:33,708 --> 00:04:36,050
Then the experimenter strokes both hands

116
00:04:36,050 --> 00:04:37,847
with a brush at the same time.

117
00:04:38,497 --> 00:04:40,733
After a minute or two, many people start

118
00:04:40,733 --> 00:04:42,918
to feel the touch on the rubber hand.

119
00:04:43,568 --> 00:04:45,553
Some even flinch when someone threatens

120
00:04:45,553 --> 00:04:45,837
it.

121
00:04:46,487 --> 00:04:48,347
VR takes this much further.

122
00:04:48,347 --> 00:04:50,703
With a headset and motion tracking,

123
00:04:50,703 --> 00:04:52,935
researchers have made people feel

124
00:04:52,935 --> 00:04:55,043
ownership over virtual bodies —

125
00:04:55,693 --> 00:04:57,973
different heights, different ages, even

126
00:04:57,973 --> 00:05:00,307
bodies that look nothing like their own.

127
00:05:00,957 --> 00:05:03,271
Your sense of "this is me" isn't fixed.

128
00:05:03,271 --> 00:05:05,640
It's constantly being built — and it can

129
00:05:05,640 --> 00:05:06,411
be rebuilt.

130
00:05:07,061 --> 00:05:09,483
If your body can change, can your behavior

131
00:05:09,483 --> 00:05:10,237
change too?

132
00:05:10,887 --> 00:05:14,034
In 2007, Nick Yee and Jeremy Bailenson

133
00:05:14,034 --> 00:05:15,923
at Stanford tested this.

134
00:05:16,573 --> 00:05:19,215
They gave people avatars that were more or

135
00:05:19,215 --> 00:05:21,858
less attractive, or taller or shorter, and

136
00:05:21,858 --> 00:05:23,326
watched what happened.

137
00:05:23,976 --> 00:05:26,706
People with more attractive avatars walked

138
00:05:26,706 --> 00:05:29,134
closer to others in virtual space and

139
00:05:29,134 --> 00:05:31,075
shared more about themselves.

140
00:05:31,725 --> 00:05:34,471
People with taller avatars negotiated more

141
00:05:34,471 --> 00:05:36,912
aggressively than people with shorter

142
00:05:36,912 --> 00:05:37,400
ones.

143
00:05:38,050 --> 00:05:40,227
The researchers named it the Proteus

144
00:05:40,227 --> 00:05:42,676
effect, after the Greek sea god who could

145
00:05:42,676 --> 00:05:43,765
change his shape.

146
00:05:44,415 --> 00:05:46,240
We don't just wear our avatars.

147
00:05:46,240 --> 00:05:48,066
In a small way, they wear us.

148
00:05:48,716 --> 00:05:51,021
And there's one more side of virtual

149
00:05:51,021 --> 00:05:52,912
worlds that surprises people:

150
00:05:52,912 --> 00:05:54,745
they can be deeply social.

151
00:05:55,395 --> 00:05:58,143
On social VR platforms, people meet as

152
00:05:58,143 --> 00:06:01,159
avatars — to talk, dance, watch movies, or

153
00:06:01,159 --> 00:06:03,706
just sit around a virtual campfire.

154
00:06:04,356 --> 00:06:06,696
Researchers have found that people can

155
00:06:06,696 --> 00:06:09,265
feel genuine social presence with others

156
00:06:09,265 --> 00:06:11,606
in VR — the sense that another mind is

157
00:06:11,606 --> 00:06:13,033
really there with you.

158
00:06:13,683 --> 00:06:15,641
Some people say they've found their

159
00:06:15,641 --> 00:06:16,929
closest friends there.

160
00:06:16,929 --> 00:06:18,835
Some have even met their partners.

161
00:06:19,485 --> 00:06:22,275
For people who are isolated, disabled, or

162
00:06:22,275 --> 00:06:24,747
far from home, these worlds can be a

163
00:06:24,747 --> 00:06:25,508
lifeline.

164
00:06:26,158 --> 00:06:27,862
Remember the need to belong?

165
00:06:27,862 --> 00:06:30,170
It doesn't switch off when the world is

166
00:06:30,170 --> 00:06:31,160
made of pixels.

167
00:06:31,810 --> 00:06:34,222
That flexibility isn't only a weakness.

168
00:06:34,222 --> 00:06:36,519
Doctors have turned it into medicine.

169
00:06:37,169 --> 00:06:39,280
At the University of Washington,

170
00:06:39,280 --> 00:06:41,813
researchers Hunter Hoffman and David

171
00:06:41,813 --> 00:06:43,923
Patterson created a game called

172
00:06:43,923 --> 00:06:45,914
SnowWorld for burn patients.

173
00:06:46,564 --> 00:06:49,315
During painful wound care, patients threw

174
00:06:49,315 --> 00:06:51,879
snowballs at snowmen in an icy canyon.

175
00:06:52,529 --> 00:06:54,623
Many reported significantly less pain

176
00:06:54,623 --> 00:06:55,723
while they played.

177
00:06:56,373 --> 00:06:59,089
VR is also used for exposure therapy.

178
00:06:59,739 --> 00:07:02,357
People with a fear of heights, flying, or

179
00:07:02,357 --> 00:07:04,975
spiders can face their fear step by step,

180
00:07:04,975 --> 00:07:07,653
in a world where nothing can actually hurt

181
00:07:07,653 --> 00:07:08,129
them.

182
00:07:08,779 --> 00:07:11,146
It has also been used to help some

183
00:07:11,146 --> 00:07:12,553
veterans with PTSD.

184
00:07:13,203 --> 00:07:15,804
The same trick that makes you afraid of a

185
00:07:15,804 --> 00:07:18,464
virtual pit can help you stop being afraid

186
00:07:18,464 --> 00:07:19,469
of a real one.

187
00:07:20,119 --> 00:07:21,198
But there are costs.

188
00:07:21,848 --> 00:07:24,295
Many people feel cybersickness — nausea

189
00:07:24,295 --> 00:07:25,241
and dizziness.

190
00:07:25,891 --> 00:07:28,098
One common explanation is sensory

191
00:07:28,098 --> 00:07:30,603
conflict: your eyes say you're moving,

192
00:07:30,603 --> 00:07:33,108
but your inner ear says you're standing

193
00:07:33,108 --> 00:07:33,645
still,

194
00:07:34,295 --> 00:07:35,891
and your brain doesn't like the

195
00:07:35,891 --> 00:07:36,643
disagreement.

196
00:07:37,293 --> 00:07:39,457
And some effects are stranger.

197
00:07:39,457 --> 00:07:42,276
In a 2010 study, psychologist Frederick

198
00:07:42,276 --> 00:07:45,227
Aardema and colleagues found that after a

199
00:07:45,227 --> 00:07:46,736
short session in VR,

200
00:07:47,386 --> 00:07:49,935
participants reported more dissociative

201
00:07:49,935 --> 00:07:52,136
experiences — and a weaker sense of

202
00:07:52,136 --> 00:07:54,569
presence in the real world around them.

203
00:07:55,219 --> 00:07:57,219
It was a small, temporary effect.

204
00:07:57,219 --> 00:07:58,830
But it hints at something:

205
00:07:59,480 --> 00:08:02,131
if a world can feel real enough to replace

206
00:08:02,131 --> 00:08:04,606
this one for a while, coming back isn't

207
00:08:04,606 --> 00:08:05,725
always seamless.

208
00:08:06,375 --> 00:08:08,567
And this brings us to the most famous

209
00:08:08,567 --> 00:08:10,924
thought experiment about virtual worlds.

210
00:08:11,574 --> 00:08:14,689
In 1974, the philosopher Robert Nozick

211
00:08:14,689 --> 00:08:17,152
asked his readers to imagine an

212
00:08:17,152 --> 00:08:18,891
experience machine.

213
00:08:19,541 --> 00:08:22,047
Neuroscientists could plug you in and give

214
00:08:22,047 --> 00:08:24,553
you any experiences you wanted — writing a

215
00:08:24,553 --> 00:08:26,836
great novel, falling in love, making a

216
00:08:26,836 --> 00:08:27,393
friend.

217
00:08:28,043 --> 00:08:29,690
It would feel completely real.

218
00:08:29,690 --> 00:08:31,887
You'd never know you were in the machine.

219
00:08:32,537 --> 00:08:34,238
Would you plug in for the rest of your

220
00:08:34,238 --> 00:08:34,570
life?

221
00:08:35,220 --> 00:08:37,547
Nozick bet that most people would say

222
00:08:37,547 --> 00:08:39,874
no. And when philosophers ask, most

223
00:08:39,874 --> 00:08:41,037
people do refuse.

224
00:08:41,687 --> 00:08:44,134
We seem to want to actually do things,

225
00:08:44,134 --> 00:08:46,353
not just feel like we're doing them.

226
00:08:46,353 --> 00:08:48,459
To actually be someone, not just

227
00:08:48,459 --> 00:08:49,881
experience being them.

228
00:08:50,531 --> 00:08:52,122
But here's the twist.

229
00:08:52,122 --> 00:08:54,972
In 2010, philosopher Felipe De Brigard

230
00:08:54,972 --> 00:08:56,563
flipped the question.

231
00:08:57,213 --> 00:08:59,827
He told people: imagine you discover that

232
00:08:59,827 --> 00:09:02,026
you're already in the experience

233
00:09:02,026 --> 00:09:04,581
machine. Your whole life so far has been

234
00:09:04,581 --> 00:09:05,353
simulated.

235
00:09:06,003 --> 00:09:08,324
Do you want to unplug and return to your

236
00:09:08,324 --> 00:09:10,699
real life — which you know nothing about?

237
00:09:11,349 --> 00:09:12,975
Many people chose to stay.

238
00:09:13,625 --> 00:09:16,031
Maybe it isn't reality we're attached to.

239
00:09:16,031 --> 00:09:18,436
Maybe it's just the life we already have.

240
00:09:19,086 --> 00:09:20,740
So is a virtual world real?

241
00:09:21,390 --> 00:09:24,768
In his 2022 book Reality+, philosopher

242
00:09:24,768 --> 00:09:27,597
David Chalmers argues something

243
00:09:27,597 --> 00:09:29,247
surprising: yes.

244
00:09:29,897 --> 00:09:32,579
Not in the sense that a virtual chair is

245
00:09:32,579 --> 00:09:35,385
made of wood. But virtual objects are real

246
00:09:35,385 --> 00:09:36,694
digital objects.

247
00:09:37,344 --> 00:09:39,791
They exist, they have effects, and the

248
00:09:39,791 --> 00:09:42,476
experiences you have with them are genuine

249
00:09:42,476 --> 00:09:43,371
experiences.

250
00:09:44,021 --> 00:09:46,513
In his view, a life in a virtual world

251
00:09:46,513 --> 00:09:49,006
could be a meaningful life — with real

252
00:09:49,006 --> 00:09:51,498
relationships, real achievements, real

253
00:09:51,498 --> 00:09:52,167
choices.

254
00:09:52,817 --> 00:09:55,198
Virtual reality, he says, is genuine

255
00:09:55,198 --> 00:09:55,913
reality.

256
00:09:56,563 --> 00:09:57,959
Not everyone agrees.

257
00:09:57,959 --> 00:10:00,690
Critics point out that a world designed by

258
00:10:00,690 --> 00:10:03,360
someone else is also a world controlled

259
00:10:03,360 --> 00:10:04,573
by someone else —

260
00:10:05,223 --> 00:10:07,495
with its owners deciding what you see,

261
00:10:07,495 --> 00:10:09,600
what you can do, and what it costs.

262
00:10:10,250 --> 00:10:12,480
Which brings us back to Plato's cave.

263
00:10:12,480 --> 00:10:14,542
The prisoners weren't only fooled.

264
00:10:14,542 --> 00:10:16,994
Someone else was holding the objects that

265
00:10:16,994 --> 00:10:18,108
cast the shadows.

266
00:10:18,758 --> 00:10:20,183
Let's try something.

267
00:10:20,183 --> 00:10:21,794
Imagine three machines.

268
00:10:22,444 --> 00:10:24,580
The first gives you a perfect life, but

269
00:10:24,580 --> 00:10:26,361
you'll never know it's not real.

270
00:10:27,011 --> 00:10:29,129
The second gives you the same perfect

271
00:10:29,129 --> 00:10:31,247
life, but you'll always know it's a

272
00:10:31,247 --> 00:10:31,988
simulation.

273
00:10:32,638 --> 00:10:34,943
The third is just your real life — with

274
00:10:34,943 --> 00:10:37,302
all its boredom, failure, and people who

275
00:10:37,302 --> 00:10:38,839
sometimes disappoint you.

276
00:10:39,489 --> 00:10:41,016
Which would you choose?

277
00:10:41,016 --> 00:10:43,013
And here's the harder question:

278
00:10:43,013 --> 00:10:45,480
why does your answer change between the

279
00:10:45,480 --> 00:10:48,005
first and the second, if the experiences

280
00:10:48,005 --> 00:10:49,415
are exactly the same?

281
00:10:50,065 --> 00:10:52,377
Whatever you answered tells you what you

282
00:10:52,377 --> 00:10:54,743
really value. Not just feeling good — but

283
00:10:54,743 --> 00:10:56,195
something about truth.

284
00:10:56,845 --> 00:10:59,315
Ivan Sutherland imagined a room where a

285
00:10:59,315 --> 00:11:01,373
displayed bullet could be fatal.

286
00:11:01,373 --> 00:11:02,491
We're not there.

287
00:11:03,141 --> 00:11:05,272
But every year, the shadows on the wall

288
00:11:05,272 --> 00:11:06,033
get sharper.

289
00:11:06,683 --> 00:11:09,088
Maybe the real question isn't whether

290
00:11:09,088 --> 00:11:10,831
virtual worlds will become

291
00:11:10,831 --> 00:11:12,935
indistinguishable from this one.

292
00:11:13,585 --> 00:11:15,725
Maybe it's whether we'll still remember

293
00:11:15,725 --> 00:11:18,018
that we chose to walk in — and that we can

294
00:11:18,018 --> 00:11:18,884
walk back out.

295
00:11:19,534 --> 00:11:21,982
Because the most important thing about a

296
00:11:21,982 --> 00:11:24,259
cave... is knowing where the exit is.

